# Recipe: Offline-First App

## Problem

Keep the app functional offline and sync state when peers are available.

## Prerequisites

- Hypercore, Hyperbee, and Hyperswarm installed.
- Basic understanding of replication and discovery keys.

## Solution

Store state locally in Hyperbee and use Hyperswarm to replicate in the
background. Use `core.findingPeers()` and `swarm.flush()` to coordinate sync.

## Full Example

```js
import Hypercore from "hypercore";
import Hyperbee from "hyperbee";
import Hyperswarm from "hyperswarm";

const core = new Hypercore("./db");
const db = new Hyperbee(core, { keyEncoding: "utf-8", valueEncoding: "json" });
await db.ready();

// Local-first write
await db.put("profile", { name: "Ava", updatedAt: Date.now() });

// Background sync
const swarm = new Hyperswarm();
swarm.on("connection", (conn) => core.replicate(conn));
swarm.join(core.discoveryKey);

// Coordinate initial sync
const done = core.findingPeers();
await swarm.flush().then(done, done);
await core.update({ wait: true });

// Live updates
for await (const [current] of db.watch()) {
  console.log("synced version:", current.version);
}
```

## Variations

- Use Hyperdrive to cache files for offline reading and download blobs on
  demand.
- Use `core.download({ start: 0, end: -1 })` for full replication when online.

## Troubleshooting

- If updates do not appear, ensure both peers replicate the same Hypercore and
  call `core.update({ wait: true })`.
- If sync is slow, confirm your swarm has at least one connection and consider
  prefetching with `core.download()`.
